#
# The screen, the keyboard and the mouse at boot: cadr-terminal serving the
# CADR's display over RFB, and carrying a viewer's keys and pointer back to the
# machine.  **NO OPTION IS PASSED FOR THE INPUT HALF AND NONE IS WANTED**: the
# program finds the I/O board's cables at their own page or says it could not
# and serves the screen read-only, so one init script is right for a bitstream
# with them and for one without.  It listens on port 5900, which a VNC viewer
# reaches as display :0.
#
# S85, after S80cadr-disk-packs: the drive comes present first, so the console
# log reads in the order the machine needs things.  That order is now a real
# dependency as well as a tidy one: the keyboard mapping below is a file on
# the partition S80 mounts.
#
# **IT LISTENS ON EVERY INTERFACE AND HAS NO PASSWORD.**  RFC 6143's `None` is
# the only security type this server offers, so anybody who can reach port
# 5900 on this board sees the screen.  That is deliberate and is the same
# decision the rest of this image already makes --- root logs in with the
# password `root` over Dropbear (boards/arty-z7-20/linux/buildroot/configs/arty_z7_20_defconfig)
# --- because the board is on a private LAN and the screen has to be watched
# from another machine: the board has no display of its own, and a server
# bound to the loopback address would be reachable only by somebody already
# logged in.  `--bind 127.0.0.1` is how that is changed, and then a viewer
# reaches it over an SSH tunnel.
#
# The log goes to the console, which is where the board is watched from
# (docs/boot.md).  start-stop-daemon's -b would send it to /dev/null, so the
# program is told where to write instead.
#
# **THE KEYBOARD MAPPING IS A FILE ON THE PACK PARTITION, AND IT IS
# OPTIONAL.**  S80cadr-disk-packs mounts that partition at /mnt/packs before
# this runs, and S87cadr-chaosnet reads its three settings from the same
# place for the same reason: the partition is FAT32, so a laptop with a card
# reader can edit what is on it, which is why the name ends in `.txt`.  The
# option is passed only when the file is there, because
# `--keyboard-mapping` naming a file that is not is a thing to report rather
# than a boot with no screen.  A boot where that mount failed still starts
# this and takes the built-in mapping.
#
# What goes in the file is `key` and `prefix` lines, muir's own format, and
# muir is in this image: `muir --keyboard-mapping-dump` writes the mapping
# this program already has, to edit.  docs/terminal.md has the whole of it.

PORT=5900
PIDFILE=/var/run/cadr-terminal.pid
PACKS=/mnt/packs
KEYS_FILE="$PACKS/terminal.keyboard.mapping.txt"

start() {
	set --
	[ -f "$KEYS_FILE" ] && set -- --keyboard-mapping "$KEYS_FILE"

	printf "Starting cadr-terminal: "
	start-stop-daemon -S -q -b -m -p "$PIDFILE" \
		--exec /usr/bin/cadr-terminal -- --port "$PORT" "$@" --log /dev/console
	[ $? = 0 ] && echo "OK" || echo "FAIL"
}

stop() {
	printf "Stopping cadr-terminal: "
	start-stop-daemon -K -q -p "$PIDFILE"
	[ $? = 0 ] && echo "OK" || echo "FAIL"
}

case "$1" in
	start) start ;;
	stop) stop ;;
	restart|reload) stop; start ;;
	*) echo "Usage: $0 {start|stop|restart}"; exit 1 ;;
esac

exit $?
